      "text": "I've now exhausted my available search and retrieval options, and I want to be fully transparent with you about what I found — and didn't find.\n\n---\n\n## What the tools returned\n\nEvery search and page fetch came back either empty or with only the same generic high-level summary. No tool was able to retrieve:\n- A chapter-level table of contents for the 7th edition\n- Any signal transduction chapter text or section headings\n- A live, accessible online version of the book's content\n\nThis means **I cannot confirm chapter numbers, section titles, or specific signal transduction content** for the 7th edition without risking fabrication — and I won't do that for a document you're building a course around.\n\n---\n\n## What you should know about legitimate online access\n\nHere are your best real options for locating the signal transduction chapters:\n\n### 1. 📚 NCBI Bookshelf (free, but edition-limited)\nThe **4th edition** of *Molecular Biology of the Cell* is freely available in full at [ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21054/). The signal transduction chapter in that edition is **Chapter 15: Cell Signaling**. However, this is **not** the 7th edition, so chapter numbers and content will differ.\n\n### 2. 🔐 W. W. Norton Ebook / Institutional Access\nThe 7th edition (2022) is a **commercially published, copyrighted work** under W. W. Norton. Legitimate digital access routes include:\n- **Your institution's library** — check if they have a license via platforms like **VitalSource**, **RedShelf**, or a direct Norton e-book subscription\n- **Norton's instructor access portal** — if you're the course instructor, you can request complimentary instructor access at [wwnorton.com/faculty](https://wwnorton.com/faculty)\n\n### 3. 🔍 Google Books Preview\nSearch for the book on [books.google.com](https://books.google.com) — the preview often includes the table of contents and a limited number of pages, which may be enough to locate which chapter covers signal transduction and read a few pages of it.\n\n### 4. 🏫 Your Library's Print Copy\nIf your institution has adopted the text, a physical or e-reserve copy should be on hand. Signal transduction in the Alberts lineage of books has historically been a dedicated, substantial chapter (in the 6th edition it was **Chapter 15**), so the 7th edition equivalent is a reasonable starting point once you have access.\n\n---\n\nOnce you have access to the chapter, I'm happy to help you **compare its conceptual framework against your lecture notes, identify gaps, or structure a weekly syllabus around it**. Just paste the relevant passages or outline here!",
