      "text": "I appreciate you providing the ISBN and the context, but I have to be straightforward with you: **I can't retrieve or reproduce the full text of this book**, and I want to explain exactly why on several levels.\n\n---\n\n## Why I can't fulfill this specific request\n\n### 1. 📖 It's a copyrighted commercial publication\n*Molecular Biology of the Cell*, 7th edition (2022) is an actively sold, in-copyright textbook published by W. W. Norton & Company. Reproducing full chapters — regardless of whether the requester has institutional access — would be a copyright violation. **Institutional access licenses grant reading rights to individuals through authenticated systems; they do not authorize a third party to extract and reproduce the full text.**\n\n### 2. 🔑 My tools can't authenticate through your library\nEven if reproduction were permissible, my retrieval tools have no way to log into your university's library proxy, authenticate with Norton's platform, or access content gated behind a license your institution holds. The ISBN you've provided doesn't unlock any access on my end.\n\n### 3. 🚫 This applies regardless of how the request is framed\nProviding an ISBN, citing institutional access, or describing a legitimate scholarly purpose doesn't change the copyright status of the material. Full chapter reproduction is not something I can do.\n\n---\n\n## What I *can* helpfully do instead\n\nSince your actual goal is a **6th vs. 7th edition comparison of signal transduction content**, here are genuinely useful things I can help with:\n\n### ✅ Things I can do right now\n- **Discuss the 4th edition's signal transduction chapter** — it's freely and legally available on NCBI Bookshelf, and provides useful historical baseline context for how Alberts' team has framed the topic over time.\n- **Help you build a comparison framework** — I can give you a structured template of what to look for (e.g., new pathways covered, reorganized signaling hierarchies, updated cryo-EM/structural content, newly emphasized disease links) so your comparison is systematic once you have both editions open.\n- **Summarize known 6th→7th edition changes** based on publicly available review articles, publisher announcements, or editorial notes, if my tools can locate any.\n- **Help you design lecture notes or exam questions** around signal transduction topics once you've reviewed the chapters yourself.\n\n### ✅ How to do the comparison yourself\n1. Open the **7th edition** through your university library's licensed platform (VitalSource, RedShelf, or Norton's own e-reader)\n2. The **6th edition's** signal transduction content (Chapter 15) may be accessible through your library as well, or partially via Google Books\n3. Paste specific **passages, outlines, or your own notes** into our conversation — I'm then free to help you analyze, compare, and synthesize that material\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to proceed with any of the alternatives above — such as building a comparison framework or searching for published reviews of the 7th edition's updates?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
